watch-spin-heal-held — rowt #s106: a spin heal waits out the recovery cooldown.

The machine is use-auto-unchanged's with the router up (host.pid.self) and a
steady-state tick: watch.net and the proxy getters unchanged, the corp lane
already synced, the bypass list right, the auto probe answering. So the tick
reaches the healthy branch of the health check, the one place the spin heal
runs. The clash API reports no byte totals, so the traffic sample is 0 B/s.
watch.cpu names the stand-in router as @ROUTER_PID@ and its time as
@NOW-<s>@; the harness fills both. Its CPU seconds (5) and bytes (12345) are
nonzero, which a fresh sample of the stand-in never is, so a sample left
unwritten shows in the tree; the compare masks the pid and this run's own
stamps only (snap_norm).

As watch-spin-idle (ROWT_CPU_SPIN_PCT=0) with a streak of 1 and no heal yet:
this second pathological tick would heal. But watch.restart is seeded in 2100,
inside the recovery cooldown on any clock, so the heal waits: watch.cpustreak
reads 2, watch.cpuheal stays unwritten (the latch is left for the heal that
runs), nothing is captured and the spin heal does not reload. The log says
"… but the last recovery was -…s ago (< 600s cooldown) — healing once that
has passed". With no heal on this tick, the upgrade check (#s104) runs as on
any healthy tick: this rowt renders what the router runs, so both sides adopt
the version (host.json.by, host.running.by).
Until #s106 both sides stamped watch.cpuheal here, and the router then spun
on for a day after a heal that never ran.
